    items: [
      ["Is travel labor more expensive than hiring locally?", "The day rate can be higher, but the right comparison is total cost. When a slow local fill lets a deadline slip, penalties, lost orders, and overtime usually cost more than the travel crew would have. AFIMAC scopes the full cost against the risk avoided during the consultation."],
      ["When should I use travel labor instead of local hiring?", "When the local market can’t supply the skills fast enough, the gap is large or time-critical, and you need a crew productive in days rather than weeks. If time is on your side and there are people to hire locally, local hiring is usually the better call."],
      ["Contract or permanent: which is right for a temporary gap?", "For a gap with a clear beginning and end (a launch, backlog, or changeover), contract labor is the cleaner fit. You carry the crew only while you need it and demobilize when your permanent workforce recovers, with no long-term cost left on the books."],
      ["How fast can CSTL move compared to the alternatives?", "Direct hiring typically runs 45 to 90 days to a productive crew and an agency 14 to 35 days. For most roles, CSTL is on site within days of a signed plan."],
